    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>No clinical trial has measured under-eye darkness as an outcome for an oral collagen supplement, so collagen cannot honestly be sold as a treatment for dark circles.</strong> Collagen peptide trials have measured crow's feet depth and skin elasticity instead. Dark circles are usually pigment, visible blood vessels, hollowing or a mix of all three, and the type decides the treatment.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The evidence gap:</strong> zero randomised trials of oral collagen have used under-eye darkness as an endpoint, in either direction.</li>
        <li><strong>Most are inherited:</strong> in 200 Indian dermatology patients, 51.5% of cases were constitutional and 63% had a family history.</li>
        <li><strong>What does work:</strong> in a 2025 meta-analysis, 82% of patients treated with lasers reached more than 50% improvement. Platelet-rich plasma reached 44%.</li>
        <li><strong>An honest limitation:</strong> a multicentre trial of oral glutathione measured the under-eye area and the difference against placebo was not statistically significant.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

