      <h2 id="what-causes">What actually causes dark circles under the eyes?</h2>
      <p>Four different things produce the same appearance, which is why one product cannot fix all of them. Dermatology reviews sort dark circles into pigmented, vascular, structural and mixed types, and the treatment is chosen from that classification rather than from the symptom.</p>

      <p><strong>Pigment.</strong> Extra melanin sits in the skin itself. This is the most common form in Indian and other deeper skin tones, and it is often simply inherited. A study of 200 patients at an Indian dermatology outpatient department, published in the <em>Indian Journal of Dermatology</em> in 2014, found the constitutional type in 51.5% of cases and a post-inflammatory type in a further 22.5%; 63% had a family history.</p>

      <p><strong>Blood vessels.</strong> The skin of the lower eyelid is among the thinnest on the body. Where it is thin and translucent, the dark venous blood underneath shows through as a blue or purple cast. Nothing is actually pigmented here; you are seeing anatomy.</p>

      <p><strong>Shadow.</strong> Volume loss at the tear trough and laxity of the eyelid skin create a hollow, and the hollow casts a shadow. This is the type that worsens with age and with rapid weight loss, and it is the one most often mistaken for a stain. The same mechanism is at work in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-loose-skin">loose skin after weight loss</a>, where the limiting factor turns out to be elastin rather than collagen.</p>

      <p><strong>Mixed.</strong> Most adults have two or three of these at once. The 2014 series found the lower eyelids involved in 72.5% of patients, peaking in the 16 to 25 age group at 47.5% of cases — young enough that ageing is clearly not the main driver.</p>

      <p>Two treatable contributors deserve naming. Allergic rhinitis produces so-called allergic shiners: a case-control study in the <em>Journal of Allergy and Clinical Immunology</em> compared 126 children with allergic rhinitis against 123 controls and found the shiners darker and larger in the allergic group, with darkness tracking the duration of the rhinitis. A published letter responding to it noted that 24% of healthy controls had significant shiners too, against 76% of allergic children — so the sign is suggestive, not diagnostic. An Indian school survey in Jaipur put allergic rhinitis prevalence at 33.5%, so this is not a rare cause here.</p>

      <p>And sleep, where the evidence is weaker than everyone assumes. Sundelin and colleagues, in <em>SLEEP</em> in 2013, found observers rated faces as having darker under-eye circles after 31 hours without sleep. But a 2017 study in the <em>Journal of Cosmetics, Dermatological Sciences and Applications</em> that measured the area instrumentally found no statistically significant change in L*, the lightness value. Sleep loss reliably changes how tired you look. Whether it changes the pigment is unclear.</p>

