      <h2 id="does-collagen-help">Does collagen help dark circles?</h2>
      <p>There is no evidence that it does, and we sell collagen. The trials that exist measured something else.</p>

      <p>Oral collagen peptides have been tested against eye-area <em>wrinkles</em> several times. A randomised, placebo-controlled trial of a fish collagen drink published in the <em>Journal of Cosmetic Dermatology</em> in 2021 reported crow's feet depth falling 14% at four weeks and 14.9% at eight weeks from baseline, while the control group got slightly worse; it enrolled 50 participants. Wrinkle depth and skin darkness are not the same measurement, and no trial has made the jump from one to the other.</p>

      <p>The wider picture also got harder in 2025, and we would rather you heard it from us. Myung and Park's meta-analysis in <em>The American Journal of Medicine</em> pooled 23 randomised trials and about 1,474 participants. Across all trials, collagen significantly improved hydration, elasticity and wrinkles — but split by funding, only the industry-funded trials showed those benefits, the non-industry-funded ones showed no effect on any of the three, and the authors concluded there is currently no clinical evidence supporting collagen for skin ageing. A letter in the same journal disputed that subgroup analysis, arguing several trials counted as non-funded were manufacturers' internal research. Both are on the record. Our fuller account is in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>.</p>

      <p>The one slice of the dark-circle problem where collagen is even mechanistically plausible is the structural one: thicker, better-hydrated eyelid skin would in principle transmit less venous blue and sit less hollow. Plausible is not measured. Nobody has run that trial.</p>

      <p>What about the pigmentation actives that travel with collagen? Oral glutathione has real evidence at a whole-face level: a 2025 systematic review in the <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em> found significant melanin-index reductions against placebo across five randomised trials at 250mg once or twice daily and 500mg once daily. The trial that looked at the under-eye area specifically is the inconvenient one — a multicentre randomised controlled trial in Indonesia tested oral glutathione with ascorbic acid, alpha-lipoic acid and zinc and measured five facial zones including beneath the eye. The glutathione group did better on skin tone and spot measures, but the difference against placebo was not statistically significant. Dose detail is in <a href="/pages/glutathione-for-skin">glutathione for skin</a>.</p>

      <p>Oral niacinamide sits in the same place: topical niacinamide has pigmentation evidence, and we could find no randomised trial of swallowed niacinamide with a periorbital endpoint. The same goes for swallowed hyaluronic acid, where the <a href="/pages/oral-hyaluronic-acid">published trials measure whole-face hydration and wrinkle depth</a>, not under-eye tone.</p>

      <h2 id="which-type">How do you tell which type you have?</h2>
      <p>There is a test you can do in front of a mirror in ten seconds, and it changes what you should spend money on. It is the eyelid stretch test, used in the 2014 Indian study alongside Wood's lamp examination.</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Stand in even, front-on light. Avoid overhead downlights, which manufacture shadows.</li>
        <li>Place a fingertip at the outer corner of one eye and gently stretch the lower eyelid skin sideways. Do not pull hard, and do not drag the eye itself.</li>
        <li><strong>If the darkness fades while stretched</strong>, you are looking at shadow from laxity or a tear trough. Flattening the skin removed the hollow.</li>
        <li><strong>If the darkness deepens</strong>, it is vascular. Stretching thinned the skin further and let more venous blue show.</li>
        <li><strong>If nothing changes</strong>, it is pigment sitting in the skin itself.</li>
        <li>Repeat on the other side. Asymmetry is common and normal.</li>
      </ol>

