      <h2 id="what-works">What actually works for dark circles?</h2>
      <p>Procedures carry the evidence, and the newest pooled analysis puts them in a clear order. The first systematic review and meta-analysis of dark eye circle treatments, published in <em>Dermatologic Therapy</em> in 2025, reported the share of patients achieving excellent or good improvement — defined as more than 50% — by treatment: lasers 82%, combination treatments 75%, chemical peels 63%, carboxytherapy 54% and platelet-rich plasma 44%.</p>

      <p>A 2020 systematic review of 39 studies adds the matching logic: fillers and autologous fat grafting where volume loss is the cause, blepharoplasty where laxity is marked, topical creams and peels where the problem is pigment, and lasers rated mildly to moderately beneficial for both vascular and pigmented types. Its authors cautioned that the underlying evidence is low quality and the recommendations should be read selectively. Peels around the eye also carry real risks, including hypopigmentation and ocular injury. These belong in a dermatologist's chair, not on a shopping list.</p>

      <p>Among injectables, a split-site randomised trial in the <em>Journal of Cosmetic Dermatology</em> in 2024 compared platelet-rich plasma on one side of the face against tranexamic acid plus vitamin C mesotherapy on the other, over three sessions three weeks apart. Both sides improved significantly and the two were equal — a useful result from a small trial.</p>

      <p>Do the free things first: daily sunscreen on the eyelid skin and sunglasses, which matter in Indian sun; treating allergic rhinitis if you have it; sleeping enough; and leaving the area alone, since rubbing is how post-inflammatory pigment accumulates. For the broader groundwork, see <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">how to boost collagen naturally</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="blood-tests">Should you get blood tests first?</h2>
      <p>If your dark circles arrived recently or came with fatigue, get your haemoglobin and ferritin checked before you buy anything. Low haemoglobin makes the skin paler and the vessels beneath it more visible, and the association turns up repeatedly in Indian data: a clinico-epidemiological study of periocular hyperpigmentation found iron deficiency anaemia in 61 of 130 patients, or 46.9%, and a widely cited review attributes a figure near 50% to Indian work.</p>

      <p>Two honest caveats: the association is observational, not causal, and most people with dark circles have entirely normal iron studies. No large trial has shown that correcting a deficiency reliably clears the circles. The reason to test is that iron deficiency matters on its own terms, whatever your under-eyes do afterwards. Do not start iron without a ferritin result.</p>

      <p><strong>See a doctor if</strong> the darkness appeared suddenly or is getting worse quickly; if it is on one side only; if there is swelling, redness, pain or a change in vision; if you also have heavy periods, breathlessness, unusual fatigue or pica; if the whites of your eyes or your skin look yellow, since the 2014 series linked jaundice to periocular pigmentation in 16% of subjects; or if there is persistent nasal blockage, sneezing or itching that points at untreated allergy. Thyroid disease, liver disease and chronic illness all appeared among the associations in that series, which is the real argument for a consultation rather than a cream.</p>

