      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help dark circles under the eyes?</summary>
          <p>There is no trial evidence that it does. Oral collagen trials have measured skin hydration, elasticity and eye-area wrinkle depth, never under-eye darkness. The only type of dark circle where collagen is even mechanistically plausible is the structural, shadow-casting type, and that has not been tested either.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long would it take to see any change in my under-eyes?</summary>
          <p>Nobody can give you an honest number, because the outcome has never been measured in a trial. Collagen skin trials generally run 8 to 12 weeks for hydration and elasticity changes. If a brand promises visible under-eye results in a fixed number of weeks, it is quoting a figure that does not exist.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are dark circles genetic?</summary>
          <p>Often, yes. In 200 patients studied at an Indian dermatology department, 51.5% of cases were the constitutional type and 63% reported a family history. Constitutional dark circles tend to appear young and persist regardless of sleep and diet, which is why they frustrate people who have fixed everything else.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will sleeping more get rid of my dark circles?</summary>
          <p>It will make you look less tired, which is not the same thing. Observers rated faces as having darker under-eye circles after 31 hours without sleep in a 2013 study in SLEEP, but a 2017 study that measured under-eye lightness instrumentally found no statistically significant change. Sleep is worth fixing for its own sake; it will not clear inherited pigment.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does the glutathione in a collagen shot lighten dark circles?</summary>
          <p>Oral glutathione has evidence for whole-face pigmentation at 250mg to 500mg daily, but the one randomised trial that measured the under-eye area specifically found the difference against placebo was not statistically significant. Collabloom contains 250mg of glutathione, which is within the studied range for tone and pigmentation generally, not for dark circles.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the most effective treatment for dark circles?</summary>
          <p>It depends on the cause, but procedures outperform anything you can swallow. A 2025 meta-analysis found 82% of patients reached more than 50% improvement with lasers and 75% with combination treatments, against 63% for chemical peels and 44% for platelet-rich plasma. Fillers suit dark circles caused by hollowing rather than pigment.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can anaemia cause dark circles?</summary>
          <p>It is associated with them. An Indian study found iron deficiency anaemia in 61 of 130 patients with periocular hyperpigmentation, or 46.9%, and the usual explanation is that paler skin makes the vessels underneath more visible. The link is observational, most people with dark circles have normal iron levels, and you should not start iron without a ferritin result.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do eye creams with collagen in them work?</summary>
          <p>Collagen is a large structural protein and does not cross intact skin in any meaningful quantity, so a collagen eye cream works mainly as a moisturiser. Moisturising can soften the look of fine lines temporarily. If you want a topical with pigmentation evidence behind it, that is a conversation for a dermatologist, not a collagen label.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

